---
// Sources: Lunaaaalj/GeoStats: README.md, docs/reporte/reporte.tex (method, diagnostics,
// validation), docs/presentacion/index.html speaker notes (2027 central forecast) and
// docs/presentacion/datos.json (the grid). Nothing here is stated beyond those documents.
import Project from '../layouts/Project.astro';
import TeX from '../components/TeX.astro';
import HexMapFigure from '../components/HexMapFigure.astro';
---
<Project
  slug="geostats"
  abstract="A geostatistical analysis of the 379,294 traffic incidents recorded by INEGI in the 18 municipalities of the Monterrey metropolitan area between 2019 and 2024, and a hierarchical Bayesian model that forecasts incidents for each one-kilometre cell. Risk is concentrated and persistent: 5% of the cells hold 44% of incidents, and the 2019 ranking of cells correlates 0.83 with that of 2024."
>
  <section class="fig-section" aria-label="Figure 1">
    <HexMapFigure />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="problem-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="problem-h">Problem</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          The project asks three questions of the metropolitan area’s traffic incidents: where risk concentrates, who
          bears its cost, and how well it can be anticipated. It was developed for the Semana de la Movilidad by
          Angel Eduardo Luna, Jenaro Alcaraz, Roberto Pérez, Damián Yul Reynoso and Mateo Rodolfo Flores.
        </p>
        <p>
          The human cost is the exception, but a heavy one. 92.9% of incidents cause material damage only; the rest
          left 25,642 people injured and 1,167 dead, and 64.4% of those deaths occurred at intersections in four
          municipalities: Monterrey, Apodaca, Guadalupe and García. Where incidents are most frequent is not where they
          are most severe: central cells average 498 incidents and 4.7% leave casualties, peripheral cells 29 and
          8.4%. A ranking by count alone puts the periphery last.
        </p>
        <p>
          The original question, how many incidents and in which census tracts, was reformulated before any data was
          touched. If counts per unit are independent Poisson, the total and its distribution across units come from
          one model, so a single model of the rate per unit answers both “how many” and “where”.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>No model can predict a single crash. What can be estimated is how loaded the dice are in each place: the expected rate. The ranking, the total and the map all follow from it.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="data-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="data-h">Data and spatial unit</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          The source is INEGI’s <i>Accidentes de Tránsito Terrestre en Zonas Urbanas y Suburbanas</i> (ATUS), a census
          of the reports filed by each municipality’s traffic police, not a survey. The records are cut to the 18
          municipalities of the metropolitan area and assigned to a grid of regular hexagons 500 m a side
          (0.65 km²). The 2,241 cells with at least one incident in six years are kept.
        </p>
        <p>
          The structure is strong. The Gini coefficient of counts per cell is 0.766, and Moran’s <TeX tex="I" /> on <TeX tex={String.raw`\log(1+n)`} /> is 0.648. Local statistics are corrected for multiple testing
          (Benjamini–Hochberg): significant LISA cells fall from 682 to 287, and what survives is a compact core over
          the consolidated urban area with no isolated hot spots. Risk follows corridors, not corners.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>Hexagons rather than census tracts: tract boundaries run along the avenues, exactly where incidents happen, and tracts vary widely in size. Equal cells can be compared directly.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="form-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="form-h">Formulation</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          For cell <TeX tex="i" /> in municipality <TeX tex="j(i)" /> and year <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex="a" />,</span> the count of recorded
          incidents is negative binomial, with a municipal effect that changes over time and a spatial effect for each
          cell.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Likelihood</p>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`Y_{ia} \sim \mathrm{NB}(\mu_{ia},\,\theta), \qquad \log \mu_{ia} = \beta_0 + m_{j(i),a} + u_i`} /><span class="eqn">(1)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>The municipal term absorbs how much each municipality reports; the cell term absorbs where, within the municipality, incidents concentrate.</p></aside>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Municipal level</p>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`m_{j,2019} = \sigma_m z_j, \qquad m_{j,a} = m_{j,a-1} + \sigma_{rw}\,\varepsilon_{j,a}, \quad \varepsilon_{j,a} \sim t_3`} /><span class="eqn">(2)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>A random walk with heavy-tailed steps: most municipalities move little from year to year, and a few jump. The <TeX tex="z_j" /> sum to zero, which identifies <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`\beta_0`} />.</span></p></aside>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Cell level</p>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`\mathbf{u} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{0},\ \sigma_u^2\,(D - \alpha A)^{-1}\right)`} /><span class="eqn">(3)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>Each cell borrows strength from its six neighbours: <TeX tex="A" /> is the adjacency of the hexagons, <TeX tex="D" /> their number of neighbours, and <TeX tex={String.raw`\alpha`} /> how strongly neighbours resemble each other.</p></aside>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          A BYM2 spatial term was tried first, in both parameterisations, and did not identify: with five years of
          counts per cell the data fix each <TeX tex="u_i" /> almost completely, and only the priors can split it into
          spatial and independent parts. The proper CAR prior has a single variance and a single <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`\alpha`} />,</span> and is identified by strong data.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="method-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="method-h">Method</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          The model is fitted in PyMC on an annual cell-by-year panel of 11,205 rows, with four NUTS chains (nutpie),
          1,000 warm-up iterations and 2,000 draws, in four minutes. There are no divergences; over the 11,205 identified
          quantities <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`\log \mu_{ia}`} />,</span> the largest <TeX tex={String.raw`\hat{R}`} /> is 1.005
          and the smallest effective sample size 2,441. With alternative priors the hyperparameters change only in the
          third decimal.
        </p>
        <p>
          Validation is out of sample: the model is trained on 2019–2023 and asked for 2024, which it has not seen, and
          compared with three baselines on mean absolute error, continuous ranked probability score (CRPS), the coverage
          of the 90% interval, and the predictive accuracy index (PAI) of the top 5% of cells.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>CRPS scores the whole forecast distribution, not just its centre. Coverage asks whether a 90% interval contains the truth 90% of the time.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="results-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="results-h">Results</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <table class="data">
          <caption class="vh">Validation on 2024, cell-by-year scale.</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col" class="n">MAE</th><th scope="col" class="n">CRPS</th><th scope="col" class="n">90% coverage</th><th scope="col" class="n">PAI 5%</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Mean, 2019–2023</th><td class="n">9.59</td><td class="n">8.04</td><td class="n">63%</td><td class="n">8.41</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Mean, 2022–2023</th><td class="n">8.15</td><td class="n">6.56</td><td class="n">68%</td><td class="n">8.38</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Negative binomial, recent history</th><td class="n">8.18</td><td class="n">6.93</td><td class="n">90%</td><td class="n">8.38</td></tr>
            <tr class="best"><th scope="row">Hierarchical, CAR and municipality</th><td class="n">8.19</td><td class="n">6.17</td><td class="n">87%</td><td class="n">8.42</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">Validation on 2024, from the project’s technical report.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>The hierarchical model predicted 70,815 incidents for 2024 against 71,249 recorded in the grid’s cells: 0.6% off the metropolitan total.</p></aside>
    </div>
    <div class="r after-table">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          The hierarchical model ties the best baseline on absolute error and beats it on CRPS by 6%: its centre points
          where a well-chosen average already points, but its distribution is better calibrated. The baseline’s 90%
          interval covers 68% of cells; the model’s covers 87%. Spatial dependence net of the municipality is strong, <TeX tex={String.raw`\alpha = 0.85`} /> (94% interval 0.81 to 0.88). The central forecast for 2027 is 73,577
          incidents, with a margin of 3%.
        </p>
        <p>
          The PAI of 8.4 did not move in any model, from the first spatial analysis to the hierarchical one: choosing
          5% of cells from the 2019–2023 data captures 42% of 2024’s incidents by counting alone. Where incidents happen
          is decided by history; changing that would need covariates that vary by cell, such as road exposure, land use
          or population.
        </p>
        <h3>Limits</h3>
        <p>
          The dominant source of error is administrative, not statistical. Municipalities change how they report from
          one year to the next: Santa Catarina recorded 46 incidents in 2021 and 1,813 in 2022; Guadalupe, 7,868 in
          2020 and 2,770 in 2024. The municipal effect therefore measures reporting, not risk, and no model can
          anticipate a change of reporting regime from earlier data. The forecasts are conditional on no such change,
          and their intervals are a floor on the real uncertainty.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>Toggle 2021 and 2022 in Fig. 1 and watch Santa Catarina, west of the centre, appear: the city did not change in a year; its reporting did.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</Project>

<style>
  .fig-section { margin-top: 56px; }
  .after-table { margin-top: 28px; }
  .table-note { margin: 8px 0 0; font: 400 14px/1.45 var(--sans); color: var(--graphite); }
  .best th, .best td { font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); }
</style>
